   "id": "module-1-signal-foundations-analog-vs-digital-systems",
   "title": "Module 1: Signal Foundations & Analog vs. Digital Systems",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "t": "h3",
     "text": "1.1 The Nature of Signals: Time vs. Amplitude Discretization"
    },
    {
     "t": "p",
     "text": "In electrical engineering, signals represent physical variables carrying information. A signal is mathematically categorized across two orthogonal dimensions:"
    },
    {
     "t": "ol",
     "items": [
      "**Time Domain**: Continuous-Time (CT) vs. Discrete-Time (DT).",
      "**Amplitude Domain**: Continuous-Amplitude (CA) vs. Discrete-Amplitude (DA)."
     ]
    },
    {
     "t": "p",
     "text": "This gives rise to the four fundamental quadrants of signals (Slides 1\u20132):"
    },
    {
     "t": "table",
     "header": [
      "Signal Category",
      "Time Domain",
      "Amplitude Domain",
      "Physical Real-World Example"
     ],
     "align": [
      ":---",
      ":---",
      ":---",
      ":---"
     ],
     "rows": [
      [
       "**Continuous-Time Analog**",
       "Continuous ($t \\in \\mathbb{R}$)",
       "Continuous ($x(t) \\in \\mathbb{R}$)",
       "Natural human voice, analog microphone output, thermal sensor voltage"
      ],
      [
       "**Discrete-Time Analog**",
       "Discrete ($n \\in \\mathbb{Z}$)",
       "Continuous ($x[n] \\in \\mathbb{R}$)",
       "Sample-and-Hold (S/H) output prior to ADC quantization"
      ],
      [
       "**Continuous-Time Digital**",
       "Continuous ($t \\in \\mathbb{R}$)",
       "Discrete (Quantized Levels)",
       "Physical TTL/CMOS digital signal on a PCB trace with continuous timeline"
      ],
      [
       "**Discrete-Time Digital**",
       "Discrete ($n \\in \\mathbb{Z}$)",
       "Discrete (Binary bits)",
       "Register contents inside a digital signal processor (DSP) or CPU"
      ]
     ]
    },
    {
     "t": "alert",
     "type": "IMPORTANT",
     "title": null,
     "text": "**Faculty Golden Rule**:  \nA signal is classified as **Analog** if its amplitude is *continuous* (can take uncountably infinite values within its dynamic range).  \nA signal is classified as **Digital** if and only if its amplitude is *quantized / discrete* (restricted to a finite set of allowable voltage bands).  \nTime discretization alone does NOT make a signal digital!"
    },
    {
     "t": "h3",
     "text": "1.2 The Quantization Process & Staircase Generation"
    },
    {
     "t": "p",
     "text": "When an analog voltage signal ranging continuously from $0\\text{ V}$ to $5\\text{ V}$ is processed by an analog-to-digital converter (ADC), its amplitude is partitioned into discrete voltage bands (Slide 3):"
    },
    {
     "t": "ul",
     "items": [
      "$0\\text{ V} \\le V < 1\\text{ V} \\implies \\text{Level } L_1$",
      "$1\\text{ V} \\le V < 2\\text{ V} \\implies \\text{Level } L_2$",
      "$2\\text{ V} \\le V < 3\\text{ V} \\implies \\text{Level } L_3$",
      "$3\\text{ V} \\le V < 4\\text{ V} \\implies \\text{Level } L_4$",
      "$4\\text{ V} \\le V \\le 5\\text{ V} \\implies \\text{Level } L_5$"
     ]
    },
    {
     "t": "p",
     "text": "The resulting quantized waveform exhibits a characteristic **staircase trajectory**. Each continuous segment is mapped to its assigned representative discrete level, establishing an initial quantization error bounded by half the step size ($\\pm \\Delta/2$)."
    },
    {
     "t": "h3",
     "text": "1.3 Noise Immunity: Why Digital Logic Dominates Analog Systems"
    },
    {
     "t": "p",
     "text": "The fundamental reason why modern computing is universally digital rather than analog is **Noise Margin & Noise Immunity** (Slide 4)."
    },
    {
     "t": "p",
     "text": "Consider a transmission channel where random electrical noise $v_{\\text{noise}} = +0.03\\text{ V}$ is superimposed onto a signal $V_{\\text{signal}} = 1.72\\text{ V}$:"
    },
    {
     "t": "ul",
     "items": [
      "**In an Analog System**:"
     ]
    },
    {
     "t": "math",
     "tex": "V_{\\text{received}} = V_{\\text{signal}} + v_{\\text{noise}} = 1.72\\text{ V} + 0.03\\text{ V} = 1.75\\text{ V}"
    },
    {
     "t": "p",
     "text": "Because every voltage value carries unique informational meaning in an analog system, $1.75\\text{ V}$ is interpreted as a completely different value from $1.72\\text{ V}$. The error is permanent and accumulates cascaded across every amplification stage."
    },
    {
     "t": "ul",
     "items": [
      "**In a Digital System**:"
     ]
    },
    {
     "t": "p",
     "text": "Level $L_2$ represents the entire allowable band $[1.0\\text{ V}, 2.0\\text{ V})$.\n  When $1.72\\text{ V}$ is corrupted to $1.75\\text{ V}$, $1.75\\text{ V}$ still resides comfortably inside the band $[1.0\\text{ V}, 2.0\\text{ V})$.  \n  The receiver's regenerative threshold circuit rounds it back to **Level $L_2$** without loss of a single bit of information!"
    },
    {
     "t": "h3",
     "text": "1.4 Binary Systems & Advantages of Digital Implementation"
    },
    {
     "t": "p",
     "text": "In a **binary digital system** (Slide 5), the continuum is compressed into exactly two distinct states:"
    },
    {
     "t": "ul",
     "items": [
      "**Logic '1' (HIGH)**: Typically $+5\\text{ V}$ in TTL, $+3.3\\text{ V}/+1.8\\text{ V}$ in modern CMOS.",
      "**Logic '0' (LOW)**: Typically $0\\text{ V}$ (Ground)."
     ]
    },
    {
     "t": "p",
     "text": "**Core Advantages of Digital Design**:"
    },
    {
     "t": "ol",
     "items": [
      "**Exceptional Noise Immunity**: Large allowable noise margins between logic bands prevent spurious false transitions.",
      "**Lossless Storage & Reproduction**: Digital binary patterns (`0`s and `1`s) can be stored indefinitely in magnetic, optical, or semiconductor memory without degradation.",
      "**Programmability & Precision**: Processing precision is simply a function of word length (number of bits), governed strictly by mathematics rather than component tolerances (resistor drift, transistor aging).",
      "**Integration Density (VLSI)**: Digital CMOS gates dissipate near-zero static power, allowing billions of transistors to be fabricated monolithically onto a single silicon die."
     ]
    }
   ]
