   "id": "module-2-digital-pulse-parameters-waveform-metrics-clocks",
   "title": "Module 2: Digital Pulse Parameters, Waveform Metrics & Clocks",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "t": "h3",
     "text": "2.1 The Real-World Digital Pulse"
    },
    {
     "t": "p",
     "text": "In elementary logic, a square pulse transitions instantaneously between $0\\text{ V}$ and $V_H$. In real physical circuits, parasitic capacitance, gate capacitance, and finite transistor on-resistance create exponential charge/discharge curves (Slides 6\u20137)."
    },
    {
     "t": "img",
     "src": "/notes/de/figures/fig_01_pulse_metrics.png",
     "alt": "Digital Pulse Parameters"
    },
    {
     "t": "p",
     "text": "The standard IEEE definitions for pulse metrics are defined as follows:"
    },
    {
     "t": "ol",
     "items": [
      "**Rise Time ($t_r$)**:"
     ]
    },
    {
     "t": "ul",
     "items": [
      "The time required for the leading (rising) edge of the pulse to transition from **10%** of its steady-state amplitude to **90%** of its steady-state amplitude:"
     ]
    },
    {
     "t": "math",
     "tex": "t_r = t_{90\\%} - t_{10\\%}"
    },
    {
     "t": "ol",
     "items": [
      "**Fall Time ($t_f$)**:"
     ]
    },
    {
     "t": "ul",
     "items": [
      "The time required for the trailing (falling) edge of the pulse to transition from **90%** of its steady-state amplitude down to **10%** of its steady-state amplitude:"
     ]
    },
    {
     "t": "math",
     "tex": "t_f = t_{10\\%} - t_{90\\%}"
    },
    {
     "t": "ol",
     "items": [
      "**Pulse Width ($t_w$ or $T_{pw}$)**:"
     ]
    },
    {
     "t": "ul",
     "items": [
      "The duration of the active state measured strictly between the **50% amplitude points** of the rising and falling edges:"
     ]
    },
    {
     "t": "math",
     "tex": "t_w = t_{\\text{falling}, 50\\%} - t_{\\text{rising}, 50\\%}"
    },
    {
     "t": "h3",
     "text": "2.2 Periodic Waveform Metrics & Duty Cycle"
    },
    {
     "t": "p",
     "text": "For any periodic pulse train (Slides 8\u201310):"
    },
    {
     "t": "ul",
     "items": [
      "**Total Period ($T$)**: The time duration required to complete one full cycle:"
     ]
    },
    {
     "t": "math",
     "tex": "T = T_{\\text{HIGH}} + T_{\\text{LOW}}"
    },
    {
     "t": "ul",
     "items": [
      "**Cyclic Frequency ($f$)**: The number of complete cycles per second:"
     ]
    },
    {
     "t": "math",
     "tex": "f = \\frac{1}{T}\\quad [\\text{Hz}]"
    },
    {
     "t": "ul",
     "items": [
      "**Duty Cycle**: The percentage of the total period during which the signal remains at logic HIGH:"
     ]
    },
    {
     "t": "math",
     "tex": "\\text{Duty Cycle} = \\left( \\frac{t_w}{T} \\right) \\times 100\\% = \\left( \\frac{T_{\\text{HIGH}}}{T_{\\text{HIGH}} + T_{\\text{LOW}}} \\right) \\times 100\\%"
    },
    {
     "t": "h3",
     "text": "2.3 Clock Signals vs. Serial Data Streams (The Classic GATE Trap)"
    },
    {
     "t": "p",
     "text": "In digital hardware design, differentiating between a **clock line** and a **serial data line** is vital (Slides 11\u201314)."
    },
    {
     "t": "ul",
     "items": [
      "**Clock Signal**:",
      "An electronic timing signal oscillating continuously between HIGH and LOW at a constant frequency.",
      "**Ideal Clock Duty Cycle**: Exactly **50%** ($T_{\\text{HIGH}} = T_{\\text{LOW}} = T/2$).",
      "Used strictly as a reference to synchronize flip-flops, registers, and sequential state machines.",
      "**Serial Data Stream**:",
      "Encodes informational bit patterns (`0`s and `1`s).",
      "Bit duration is defined as $T_b$.",
      "**Data Transmission Rate**:"
     ]
    },
    {
     "t": "math",
     "tex": "\\text{Data Rate} = \\frac{1}{T_b}\\quad [\\text{bits per second (bps)}]"
    },
    {
     "t": "h4",
     "text": "High-Speed Design Example: 100 MHz Clock vs. 100 Mbps Data Stream (Slide 13)"
    },
    {
     "t": "p",
     "text": "Consider a synchronous digital transmission channel operating with a $100\\text{ MHz}$ master clock and a $100\\text{ Mbps}$ serial bit stream:"
    },
    {
     "t": "ol",
     "items": [
      "**Clock Waveform Dynamics**:"
     ]
    },
    {
     "t": "math",
     "tex": "f_{\\text{clk}} = 100\\text{ MHz} = 10^8\\text{ Hz} \\implies T_{\\text{clk}} = \\frac{1}{10^8\\text{ s}^{-1}} = 10\\text{ ns}"
    },
    {
     "t": "ul",
     "items": [
      "For a symmetrical 50% duty cycle clock:"
     ]
    },
    {
     "t": "math",
     "tex": "T_{\\text{HIGH}} = 5\\text{ ns}, \\quad T_{\\text{LOW}} = 5\\text{ ns}"
    },
    {
     "t": "ol",
     "items": [
      "**Serial Data Line Dynamics**:"
     ]
    },
    {
     "t": "math",
     "tex": "\\text{Data Rate} = 100\\text{ Mbps} = 10^8\\text{ bits/second}"
    },
    {
     "t": "math",
     "tex": "\\text{Single Bit Interval } T_b = \\frac{1}{\\text{Data Rate}} = \\frac{1}{10^8\\text{ s}^{-1}} = 10\\text{ ns}"
    },
    {
     "t": "ol",
     "items": [
      "**Bus Eye-Diagram Representation**:"
     ]
    },
    {
     "t": "ul",
     "items": [
      "In digital oscilloscope eye diagrams, serial data is mapped as consecutive data cells bounded by transition edges. Each bit cell spans exactly:"
     ]
    },
    {
     "t": "math",
     "tex": "\\Delta t = T_b = 10\\text{ ns}"
    },
    {
     "t": "ul",
     "items": [
      "**Key Physical Distinction**: Although $T_{\\text{clk}} = T_b = 10\\text{ ns}$, the clock waveform undergoes **two complete voltage transitions** within $10\\text{ ns}$ (HIGH and LOW phases), whereas a single data bit holds its single logic state across the entire $10\\text{ ns}$ window!"
     ]
    },
    {
     "t": "alert",
     "type": "CAUTION",
     "title": null,
     "text": "**Faculty Alert \u2014 The Alternating Sequence Trap (Slide 14)**:  \nWhen given an alternating pulse train that spends $2\\,\\mu\\text{s}$ HIGH and $2\\,\\mu\\text{s}$ LOW:\n- If specified as a **Clock**, Period $T = 2\\,\\mu\\text{s} + 2\\,\\mu\\text{s} = 4\\,\\mu\\text{s} \\implies f = 1/4\\,\\mu\\text{s} = 0.25\\text{ MHz}$.\n- If specified as a **Data Line**, each HIGH duration is one bit (`1`), and each LOW duration is one bit (`0`).  \n  Bit duration $T_b = 2\\,\\mu\\text{s}$!  \n  $$\\text{Data Rate} = \\frac{1}{T_b} = \\frac{1}{2 \\times 10^{-6}\\text{ s}} = 0.5\\text{ Mbps}$$  \nConfusing a data bit interval with a 2-level clock cycle is one of the most frequent point-loss traps in GATE!"
    }
   ]
